Create Group 03 — Selection Controls for the DevKit.

This group belongs to:

Layer 01 — Foundations & Interface Primitives.

Groups 01 and 02 already exist:
- Group 01 — Interaction Controls
- Group 02 — Form Controls

Use the DevKit Core Design Constitution as the immutable global foundation.

Do not redefine the global design system.

Inherit all existing:
- color tokens
- semantic colors
- typography
- spacing
- sizing
- control heights
- radius
- borders
- elevation
- shadows
- motion
- breakpoints
- responsive behavior
- accessibility rules
- focus rules
- touch-target rules
- icon system
- density system
- Light theme
- Dark theme
- High Contrast behavior
- RTL behavior
- localization behavior

Create exactly these 10 components:

021 Checkbox
022 Checkbox Group
023 Radio
024 Radio Group
025 Switch
026 Select
027 Combobox
028 Multi-Select
029 Choice Chips
030 Slider

These must be production-grade reusable primitives.

Do not create:
- settings pages
- filter panels
- preference pages
- onboarding screens
- checkout sections
- application-specific controls
- dashboard filters
- complete forms

Those must be composed from these primitives.

Every component must support appropriate:
- default
- hover
- focus
- pressed
- selected
- checked
- unchecked
- disabled
- read-only where semantically applicable
- indeterminate where applicable
- error
- warning where applicable
- success where applicable
- loading where applicable

Do not force states onto components where they are not semantically meaningful.

Selection must never depend only on color.

Every selected state must have an additional visual or semantic indication.

All components must work with:
- mouse
- keyboard
- touch
- stylus
- screen readers
- switch/accessibility navigation where applicable

All components must support:
- Light mode
- Dark mode
- High Contrast
- RTL
- localization
- dynamic text scaling
- reduced motion
- reduced transparency

Maintain the global minimum effective touch target:
44×44px.

Do not make visual controls unnecessarily large on desktop merely to satisfy the touch-target requirement. The interactive hit area may exceed the visible visual element.

Use the global control sizes:

XS = 28px
SM = 32px
MD = 40px
LG = 48px
XL = 56px

Use MD as the default control scale unless a component has a semantic reason to use another size.

Maintain consistent relationships between:
label
control
description
help text
error message
group heading

Default:
label/control relationship = 6px
control/message relationship = 6px
field-to-field spacing = 16px
related group spacing = 24px
section spacing = 32px

Do not introduce arbitrary spacing values.

Selection controls must communicate:
- what can be selected
- what is currently selected
- whether multiple choices are allowed
- whether the selection is required
- whether the control is disabled
- whether the current selection is invalid

Do not make users infer selection behavior.

Respect semantic differences:

Checkbox:
independent boolean or multi-selection.

Radio:
single choice from a mutually exclusive set.

Switch:
immediate on/off setting.

Select:
selection from a predefined list.

Combobox:
editable text combined with selectable suggestions/options.

Multi-Select:
multiple selections from a set.

Choice Chips:
compact selection/action choices.

Slider:
continuous or stepped numeric selection.

Do not duplicate these semantics simply because their visuals may appear similar.

Keyboard interaction must follow established platform conventions.

Focus must remain visible.

For composite controls, keyboard navigation must be predictable.

For menus and listboxes:
- arrow navigation
- Enter/Space selection where appropriate
- Escape dismissal
- Home/End where appropriate
must work correctly.

On mobile:
- prioritize touch usability
- avoid tiny selection targets
- transform menus into suitable touch interfaces where necessary
- prevent popovers from becoming unusably small
- account for the virtual keyboard for editable controls

On desktop:
- support keyboard navigation
- pointer hover
- contextual feedback
- efficient interaction density

All controls must gracefully handle:
- long labels
- long option names
- many options
- localized strings
- RTL text
- disabled individual options
- unavailable options
- validation messages

Do not truncate important option names without a meaningful way to discover the complete value.

Never rely on placeholder text as the only accessible label.

Selection groups must have semantic group labels.

Do not use excessive animation.

Selection feedback should be immediate and subtle.

Motion must follow the global DevKit motion tokens.

Do not add decorative gradients, excessive shadows, ornamental borders, or unnecessary glass effects.

The design should feel:
precise,
quiet,
premium,
responsive,
tactile,
and effortless.

The components should share a coherent interaction language with Groups 01 and 02 while remaining visually distinct according to their semantic purpose.

